  "response_text": "Before setting up a thread\u2019s stack, we first allocate a valid memory region to serve as the stack \u2014 in this lab, we used an array for that purpose. With a safe location ready, we then pushed values onto it that represent what the CPU expects when resuming execution: xPSR, PC (the function to run), LR, and general-purpose registers like R0\u2013R12. This process prepares a fake CPU state so the processor can \u201cresume\u201d the thread as if it had been running. Storing registers on the stack is essential for preserving the thread\u2019s context, and having a valid memory location ensures we aren\u2019t writing to or reading from unsafe or undefined memory, which could lead to crashes or hard faults.\\n",
